window.__finlexDocLoaded(10015,{"id":10015,"articles":[{"id":1000076,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"IFRS 13 defines fair value, sets out a framework for measuring fair value, and requires disclosures about fair value measurements.\n\nIt applies when another Standard requires or permits fair value measurements or disclosures about fair value measurements (and measurements based on fair value, such as fair value less costs to sell), except in specified circumstances in which other Standards govern. For example, IFRS 13 does not specify the measurement and disclosure requirements for share-based payment transactions, leases or impairment of assets. Nor does it establish disclosure requirements for fair values related to employee benefits and retirement plans.\n\nIFRS 13 defines fair value as the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date (an exit price). When measuring fair value, an entity uses the assumptions that market participants would use when pricing the asset or the liability under current market conditions, including assumptions about risk. As a result, an entity’s intention to hold an asset or to settle or otherwise fulfil a liability is not relevant when measuring fair value.","seq":0},{"id":1000077,"no":"History","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In May 2011 the International Accounting Standards Board issued IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. IFRS 13 defines fair value and replaces the requirement contained in individual Standards.\n\nOther Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IFRS 13. They include IAS 19 Employee Benefits (issued June 2011), Annual Improvements to IFRSs 2011–2013 Cycle (issued December 2013), IFRS 9 Financial Instruments (issued July 2014), IFRS 16 Leases (issued January 2016), IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (issued April 2024) and IFRS 19 Subsidiaries without Public Accountability: Disclosures (issued in May 2024).","seq":1},{"id":1000078,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related active projects]\n[Related completed projects]\nDisclosure Initiative—Targeted Standards-level Review of Disclosures\nDiscount Rates\nFair Value Measurement\nFair Value Measurement: Unit of Account (Amendments to IFRS 13)\nIFRS Taxonomy Update—Common Practice (IFRS 13)\nPost-implementation Review of IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement\nRecoverable Amount Disclosures for Non-financial Assets (Amendments to IAS 36)\nScope of Paragraph 52 (Portfolio Exception)\nShort-term Receivables and Payables (Amendments to IFRS 13)\n[Related IFRS Standards]\nIAS 40 Investment Property\nIAS 41 Agriculture\nIFRS 9 Financial Instruments\n[Related IFRIC Interpretations]\n[Unconsolidated amendments]\n[Implementation support]\nIFRS 13 supporting material","seq":2},{"id":1000079,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ifrs-13-fair-value-measurement/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf\nKPMG IFRS Accounting Toolkit (Standard Handbooks): https://kpmg.com/xx/en/what-we-do/services/audit/corporate-reporting-institute/ifrs/toolkit.html","seq":3}]});
